708:$\mu = \sum\limits_{F \in E(H)} {\mu}^F$, $ \sum\limits_{F \in E(H)} l_1^F \leq l_1$ and $\sum\limits_{F \in E(H)} l_2^F \leq l_2$.
712:Now, note that $\mu = |\widehat{\Upsilon}_{\mathbf{v}}^{-1}(V_1)|$. Thus, to show that $\mu = \sum_{F \in E(H)} {\mu}^F$, it is sufficient to show that $|\widehat{\Upsilon}_{\mathbf{v}}^{-1}(V_1)| = \sum_{F \in E(H)} {\mu}^F$. However, keeping the above argument in mind, the claim that $|\widehat{\Upsilon}_{\mathbf{v}}^{-1}(V_1)| = \sum_{F \in E(H)} {\mu}^F$ directly follows from the satisfaction of the three following conditions. We remark that the satisfaction of these conditions is a direct consequence of the supposition that $\Upsilon$ be a witnessing coloring for $y[t, {\Upsilon}^{\sigma}, \mathbf{v},\mu,l_1,l_2]=1$, together with our definition of the values ${\mu}^F, l_1^F$ and $l_2^F$.
